                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Lonely Astronaut is a very small arcade game with a pretty ridiculous idea behind it. I am controlling the corpse of a long abandoned astronaut floating around a cosmic graveyard, and I only have one button to control the broken jetpack. That is basically the entire foundation of the game, but I actually think the simplicity works well here. I am not learning complicated controls or dealing with a huge amount of systems. I am just trying to keep this dead astronaut moving through space without crashing into everything around me.\n\nThe movement is what makes it fun for me. Pressing the button gives the astronaut a burst of thrust, but I still have to deal with the momentum and direction I am already moving in. It sounds easy until I am surrounded by objects and realise I have pushed myself into a terrible position. I have to get used to making small adjustments rather than constantly holding the button down. Once I start understanding the movement, I can get into a nice rhythm where I am carefully steering through the environment and trying to stay alive for as long as possible.\n\nI also like the strange atmosphere. The whole idea of flying a dead astronaut through space could have just been a joke, but the game actually leans into the darker side of it. There is a sense of isolation to the empty space around me, while the humour keeps things from becoming too depressing. I can also unlock different skulls and use their unique properties, which gives me another reason to keep playing and experimenting with the different ways the game can work. There are 13 achievements as well, so there is a little more to chase once I have understood the main gameplay.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that there is only so much that can be done with such a limited control system. The simplicity is part of the charm, but it also means the gameplay can start feeling repetitive once I have spent enough time with it. I am still thrusting through space and trying not to crash, so there is no huge progression system or large collection of mechanics waiting for me later. I think that is fine for a quick arcade game, but it definitely limits how long I can stay interested in it at once.\n\nI also think the game works much better when I treat it as a score based arcade experience rather than expecting a traditional adventure. There is no massive campaign for me to work through and the story is mostly delivered through the strange situations and discoveries I come across. The game is also completely free, which makes the short and focused design much easier for me to appreciate. I can start it up, play for a while, try to improve what I did before and then leave without feeling like I have abandoned some huge unfinished campaign.\n\nThe hand drawn presentation fits the idea nicely too. It has a slightly grim and silly style that matches the whole concept of playing as a dead astronaut, and I like that the game does not try to make everything look overly serious. The atmosphere is helped by the emptiness of the setting because there is something oddly peaceful about floating around in space while knowing that the character I am controlling is already dead. It is a strange combination of dark humour, arcade gameplay and a little bit of existential dread.\n\nOverall, Lonely Astronaut is a simple game that I think gets a surprising amount of personality out of a very small idea. I like the awkward jetpack movement, the dark humour, the strange setting and the extra skulls that give me something to unlock. The limited controls also become the biggest weakness because there is not enough variety to keep me playing for long sessions. Still, because it is free and easy to pick up, I think it works well as a quick arcade game that I can return to whenever I want something short and unusual.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
